    "rationale": "Partial fit: the specimen clearly defines unknown-pCID opaque handling at the envelope boundary, which helps mixed-version interoperability, but it does not directly specify unknown CID-codec CAS object storage/advertise/forward behavior, and unsigned v0 weakens accountability.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit unknown-pCID behavior keeps payload bytes opaque and requires unsupported/unverified labeling.",
      "Very clear boundary between envelope dispatch and payload interpretation.",
      "Simple DAG-CBOR positional form is plausible and evolution-friendly for mixed-version peers."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The scenario is about unknown CID codecs and CAS object type binding more directly than this spec is.",
      "It does not explicitly define store, advertise, or forward rules for unknown typed objects.",
      "Base envelope signatures are absent, so provenance and integrity are externalized."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Best-effort salvage tooling could drift into unsafe or inconsistent parsing.",
      "Unsigned envelopes make spoofing or provenance loss easier.",
      "Different payload protocols may implement unknown-type fallback differently, reducing interoperability."
